\title{A Root Law for Optimal Batch Size under\\ Sub-Quadratic Multiplication}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{11 September 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Batching amortises a fixed setup cost across many candidates, but the arithmetic
that makes batching possible becomes super-linearly expensive as the batch grows.
We study the per-candidate cost
\[
C_{A,c,q,\mu}(k) \;=\; \frac{A}{k} + c + q\bigl(k^{\mu-1}-1\bigr), \qquad k \in (0,\infty),
\]
where $A>0$ is the per-batch setup, $c$ the flat per-candidate cost, $q>0$ the
multiplication-penalty scale, and $\mu$ the multiplication exponent of the
underlying big-integer arithmetic ($\mu=2$ schoolbook, $\mu=\log_2 3$ Karatsuba,
$\mu \to 1$ for FFT-based products and for the idealised flat operation model).
Our main theorem is a \emph{root law}: for every $\mu>1$ the cost has a unique
global minimiser $\kstar = \bigl(A/((\mu-1)q)\bigr)^{1/\mu}$ with value
$c-q+\mu(\mu-1)^{(1-\mu)/\mu}A^{(\mu-1)/\mu}q^{1/\mu}$, recovering the classical
square-root law at $\mu=2$ and degenerating at $\mu=1$ to a strictly decreasing
cost with no interior optimum. The proof is derivative-free, reducing to
Bernoulli's inequality through a balance principle. We further establish a
universal collapse of the cost curve to a two-parameter shape, quadratic flatness
of the optimum, multiplicative (but for $\mu<2$ not additive) convexity, strict
unimodality with the consequence that the best integer batch is a neighbour of
$\kstar$, and a root law for the batch-versus-solo crossover pool size
$\bigl(1+(s_1-c_1)/q\bigr)^{1/(\mu-1)}$. The last result explains an observed
batching reversal as a property of schoolbook arithmetic rather than of batching.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} batch amortisation, multiplication exponent, Karatsuba,
weighted AM--GM, Bernoulli's inequality, multiplicative convexity, unimodality,
smoothness testing.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The engineering situation}

Batch algorithms trade a per-batch setup cost against a super-linear per-batch
arithmetic cost. The canonical example, and the one that motivates this work, is
\emph{batch smoothness testing}: given a stream of integer candidates, decide for
each whether it factors completely over a fixed set of small primes. The batched
method forms the product of a block of $k$ candidates and tests the whole block
against a precomputed product of primes, so that one expensive setup (product
tree, modular reduction) serves $k$ candidates.

Naively, larger blocks are always better. In practice the measured cost curve turns
around: in one calibration the turn happened near $k \approx 1715$ candidates, beyond
which testing candidates one at a time was cheaper. Yet in a cost model that counts
\emph{operations} rather than \emph{machine words}, so that a multiplication costs one
tick regardless of operand size, no reversal appears at all, even at $k=512$. Two
models, two qualitatively different answers. This paper identifies the responsible
parameter: the \textbf{multiplication exponent}.

\subsection{The cost model}

Let $k>0$ denote the batch size and count cost per candidate. A fixed cost $A>0$ per
batch contributes $A/k$; a flat per-candidate cost $c$ is independent of $k$; and if
multiplying two $n$-word integers costs $\Theta(n^{\mu})$ word operations, then a
batch of $k$ candidates carries a per-candidate multiplication penalty
$\Theta(k^{\mu-1})$, normalised to $q(k^{\mu-1}-1)$ so that a batch of one is free.

\begin{definition}[Per-candidate block cost]\label{def:cost}
For $A,q>0$, $c,\mu \in \R$ and $k>0$,
\[
C_{A,c,q,\mu}(k) \;:=\; \frac{A}{k} \;+\; c \;+\; q\bigl(k^{\mu-1}-1\bigr),
\]
where $k^{\mu-1} := \exp\bigl((\mu-1)\log k\bigr)$.
\end{definition}

Relevant exponents: $\mu=2$ (schoolbook), $\mu=\log_2 3 \approx 1.585$ (Karatsuba),
$\mu = \log_3 5 \approx 1.465$ (Toom--3), and $\mu \to 1^{+}$ (FFT-based
multiplication up to logarithmic factors, and the flat operation model).

\begin{definition}[Candidate optimum and optimal value]\label{def:opt}
For $A,q>0$ and $\mu>1$,
\[
\kstar(A,q,\mu) := \left(\frac{A}{(\mu-1)q}\right)^{1/\mu}, \qquad
\Cstar(A,c,q,\mu) := c - q + \mu(\mu-1)^{\frac{1-\mu}{\mu}}A^{\frac{\mu-1}{\mu}}q^{\frac{1}{\mu}}.
\]
\end{definition}

\subsection{Contributions}

\begin{enumerate}[label=(\arabic*)]
\item The root law: $\kstar$ is the unique global minimiser of $C$ on $(0,\infty)$,
with value $\Cstar$ (Section~\ref{sec:rootlaw}).
\item A derivative-free proof via a balance principle reducing to Bernoulli's
inequality (Section~\ref{sec:engine}).
\item Degeneracies and consistency: the $\mu=2$ square-root law, the $\mu=1$
non-existence of an optimum, quantitative divergence as $\mu \downarrow 1$, and
antitonicity in $\mu$ (Section~\ref{sec:degen}).
\item Geometry of the curve: universal collapse, quadratic flatness, scaling laws,
multiplicative convexity and the explicit failure of ordinary convexity for
$\mu<2$ (Section~\ref{sec:geometry}).
\item Strict unimodality and the discrete optimum (Section~\ref{sec:discrete}).
\item A root law for the batch-versus-solo crossover pool size
(Section~\ref{sec:crossover}).
\end{enumerate}

\section{The analytic engine: Bernoulli and a balance principle}\label{sec:engine}

\begin{lemma}[Bernoulli's inequality for real exponents]\label{lem:bern}
For $u \ge 0$ and $\mu \ge 1$ we have $\mu u \le u^{\mu} + (\mu-1)$. If moreover
$\mu>1$ and $u \ne 1$, the inequality is strict.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Apply $1+\mu s \le (1+s)^{\mu}$, valid for $s \ge -1$ and $\mu \ge 1$, with
$s = u-1$, and rearrange; strictness for $s \neq 0$, $\mu>1$ is the strict form of
the same inequality.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Weighted AM--GM, normalised]\label{lem:amgm}
For $u>0$ and $\mu \ge 1$,
\[
\mu \;\le\; \frac{\mu-1}{u} + u^{\mu-1},
\]
with strict inequality whenever $\mu>1$ and $u \ne 1$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Using $u\cdot u^{\mu-1}=u^{\mu}$,
\[
\frac{\mu-1}{u}+u^{\mu-1}-\mu \;=\; \frac{u^{\mu}+(\mu-1)-\mu u}{u},
\]
and the numerator is nonnegative (resp.\ positive) by Lemma~\ref{lem:bern}.
\end{proof}

Lemma~\ref{lem:amgm} is the weighted AM--GM inequality with weights
$\bigl(\frac{\mu-1}{\mu},\frac1\mu\bigr)$ applied to $u^{-1}$ and $u^{\mu-1}$, whose
geometric mean is~$1$.

\begin{definition}[Balanced point]\label{def:balanced}
Given $q>0$, $\mu>1$ and $A>0$, a point $t>0$ is \emph{balanced} if
$A = q(\mu-1)t^{\mu}$, equivalently $\dfrac{A}{t}=(\mu-1)\,q\,t^{\mu-1}$: the
amortised setup equals $(\mu-1)$ times the marginal multiplication penalty.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Factorisation around a balanced point]\label{lem:factor}
For $t,k>0$,
\[
\frac{q(\mu-1)t^{\mu}}{k} + q\,k^{\mu-1}
\;=\; q\,t^{\mu-1}\left(\frac{\mu-1}{k/t} + \Bigl(\frac{k}{t}\Bigr)^{\mu-1}\right).
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Write $k=(k/t)\cdot t$, use $(xy)^{p}=x^{p}y^{p}$ for positive $x,y$ and
$t\cdot t^{\mu-1}=t^{\mu}$, then clear denominators.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Balance principle]\label{thm:balance}
Let $q>0$, $\mu>1$, $t>0$ and $A=q(\mu-1)t^{\mu}$. Then for all $k>0$,
\[
q\,\mu\,t^{\mu-1} \;\le\; \frac{A}{k}+q\,k^{\mu-1},
\]
with equality at $k=t$ and strict inequality for $k \ne t$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Substitute Lemma~\ref{lem:factor} and apply Lemma~\ref{lem:amgm} with $u=k/t$,
scaling by the positive factor $q\,t^{\mu-1}$. At $k=t$,
$A/t+qt^{\mu-1}=q(\mu-1)t^{\mu-1}+qt^{\mu-1}=q\mu t^{\mu-1}$.
\end{proof}

\section{The root law}\label{sec:rootlaw}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:kstar-balanced}
For $A,q>0$ and $\mu>1$, the point $\kstar=(A/((\mu-1)q))^{1/\mu}$ is positive and
balanced: $A=q(\mu-1)(\kstar)^{\mu}$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{lemma}[Equipartition at the optimum]\label{lem:equi}
With $A,q>0$ and $\mu>1$, $\dfrac{A}{\kstar}=(\mu-1)\bigl(q(\kstar)^{\mu-1}\bigr)$.
\end{lemma}

At $\mu=2$ this is the classical ``setup share equals penalty share''; as
$\mu \downarrow 1$ the setup share collapses, which is why the optimum escapes to
infinity.

\begin{lemma}[Closed form of the optimal value]\label{lem:value}
$q\,\mu\,(\kstar)^{\mu-1}
= \mu(\mu-1)^{\frac{1-\mu}{\mu}}A^{\frac{\mu-1}{\mu}}q^{\frac{1}{\mu}}$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$(\kstar)^{\mu-1}=(A/((\mu-1)q))^{(\mu-1)/\mu}$ by the power rule; split the
quotient and the product, use
$(\mu-1)^{(1-\mu)/\mu}=\bigl((\mu-1)^{(\mu-1)/\mu}\bigr)^{-1}$ and
$q/q^{(\mu-1)/\mu}=q^{1/\mu}$, the latter because $1/\mu=1-(\mu-1)/\mu$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Root law]\label{thm:rootlaw}
Let $A,q>0$ and $\mu>1$. For every $k>0$,
\[
\Cstar(A,c,q,\mu) \;\le\; C_{A,c,q,\mu}(k),
\]
with equality if and only if $k=\kstar(A,q,\mu)$. Consequently
$\{C(k):k>0\}$ has least element $\Cstar$, and there exists a \emph{unique} $t>0$
with $C(t)\le C(k)$ for all $k>0$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By Lemma~\ref{lem:kstar-balanced}, $\kstar$ is balanced, so
Theorem~\ref{thm:balance} gives $q\mu(\kstar)^{\mu-1}\le A/k+qk^{\mu-1}$ for all
$k>0$, strictly unless $k=\kstar$. Add $c-q$ to both sides and rewrite the left side
by Lemma~\ref{lem:value}. Uniqueness: any minimiser $t$ satisfies
$C(t)\le C(\kstar)=\Cstar$, which by strictness forces $t=\kstar$.
\end{proof}

Theorem~\ref{thm:rootlaw} refutes, for this cost model, the hypothesis that the
optimum might fail to be unique or fail to be of root type. Any additional structure
observed on a real machine (cache tiers, algorithm-switching thresholds) is therefore
evidence about the \emph{model}, not about the optimisation.

\section{Specialisations and degeneracies}\label{sec:degen}

\begin{proposition}[Schoolbook arithmetic]\label{prop:mu2}
$C_{A,c,q,2}(k)=A/k+c+q(k-1)$, and
$\kstar(A,q,2)=\sqrt{A/q}$, $\Cstar(A,c,q,2)=c-q+2\sqrt{Aq}$. Hence for $A,q>0$ and
$k>0$,
\[
\frac{A}{k}+c+q(k-1)=c-q+2\sqrt{Aq} \iff k=\sqrt{A/q}.
\]
\end{proposition}

\begin{proposition}[Flat model]\label{prop:mu1}
$C_{A,c,q,1}(k)=A/k+c$. For $A>0$ this is strictly decreasing, so for every $k>0$
there is $k'>k$ with $C(k')<C(k)$: no interior optimum exists.
\end{proposition}

\begin{theorem}[Divergence as $\mu \downarrow 1$]\label{thm:diverge}
Let $q>0$, $1<\mu\le2$ and $M \ge 1$. If $\mu-1 \le A/(qM^{2})$ then
$\kstar(A,q,\mu)\ge M$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The hypothesis gives $M^{2}\le A/((\mu-1)q)$. Since $M\ge1$ and $\mu\le2$ we have
$M^{\mu}\le M^{2}$, hence $M^{\mu}\le A/((\mu-1)q)$; raise to the power $1/\mu$ and
use $(M^{\mu})^{1/\mu}=M$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Antitonicity in the exponent]\label{thm:antitone}
Let $A,q>0$ and $1<\mu_1\le\mu_2$ with $(\mu_1-1)q\le A$ (equivalently
$\kstar(A,q,\mu_1)\ge1$). Then $\kstar(A,q,\mu_2)\le\kstar(A,q,\mu_1)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Put $B_i:=A/((\mu_i-1)q)$. Then $B_2\le B_1$, so $B_2^{1/\mu_2}\le B_1^{1/\mu_2}$;
and $B_1\ge1$ with $1/\mu_2\le1/\mu_1$ gives $B_1^{1/\mu_2}\le B_1^{1/\mu_1}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{example}
With $A=1000$ and $q=10^{-3}$: $\kstar(\mu=2)=1000$, while
$\kstar(\mu=3/2)=(2\cdot10^{6})^{2/3}\approx 15874$. Sub-quadratic arithmetic
strictly increases the optimal batch.
\end{example}

\section{Geometry of the cost curve}\label{sec:geometry}

\begin{definition}[Shape function]\label{def:shape}
For $\theta>0$, $S_{\mu}(\theta) := \dfrac{\mu-1}{\theta}+\theta^{\mu-1}$; note
$S_{\mu}(1)=\mu$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Universal collapse]\label{thm:collapse}
For $A,q>0$, $\mu>1$ and $\theta>0$,
\[
C_{A,c,q,\mu}\bigl(\theta\kstar\bigr)
= (c-q) + q(\kstar)^{\mu-1}\,S_{\mu}(\theta),
\]
and in particular $\Cstar=(c-q)+q(\kstar)^{\mu-1}\mu$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Apply Lemma~\ref{lem:factor} with $t=\kstar$ and $k=\theta\kstar$, so $k/t=\theta$,
and substitute the balance relation $A=q(\mu-1)(\kstar)^{\mu}$.
\end{proof}

Measured in units of the optimal batch, every batching cost curve with the same
exponent is the same curve: $A$ and $q$ enter only through the vertical scale
$q(\kstar)^{\mu-1}$ and the offset $c-q$.

\begin{theorem}[Quadratic flatness]\label{thm:flat}
For $1<\mu\le2$ and $\theta>0$,
\[
S_{\mu}(\theta) \;\le\; \mu + \frac{(\mu-1)(\theta-1)^{2}}{\theta}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Since $0 \le \mu-1 \le 1$, the reverse Bernoulli inequality gives
$\theta^{\mu-1}\le 1+(\mu-1)(\theta-1)$. Multiplying the claim by $\theta>0$ reduces
it, after collecting terms, to an elementary consequence of this bound.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Cost of mis-sizing]\label{cor:missize}
For $A,q>0$, $1<\mu\le2$ and $\theta>0$,
\[
C(\theta\kstar) \;\le\; \Cstar + q(\kstar)^{\mu-1}\cdot\frac{(\mu-1)(\theta-1)^{2}}{\theta}.
\]
\end{corollary}

In shape units the relative overhead of using $\theta\kstar$ is at most
$\frac{\mu-1}{\mu}\cdot\frac{(\theta-1)^{2}}{\theta}$: for $\mu=2$, a $20\%$ sizing
error costs at most $1.7\%$, and a factor of two at most $25\%$. The excess is
$O((\theta-1)^{2})$ and its prefactor $\mu-1$ shrinks with the exponent, so faster
arithmetic gives flatter plateaus.

\begin{proposition}[Scaling and homogeneity]\label{prop:scaling}
For $\lambda>0$, $A,q>0$, $\mu>1$:
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
\item $\kstar(\lambda A,q,\mu)=\lambda^{1/\mu}\kstar(A,q,\mu)$;
\item $\kstar(A,\lambda q,\mu)=\lambda^{-1/\mu}\kstar(A,q,\mu)$;
\item $\kstar(\lambda A,\lambda q,\mu)=\kstar(A,q,\mu)$;
\item $A_1<A_2 \implies \kstar(A_1,q,\mu)<\kstar(A_2,q,\mu)$;
\item $\Cstar(\lambda A,c,\lambda q,\mu)-(c-\lambda q)
= \lambda\bigl(\Cstar(A,c,q,\mu)-(c-q)\bigr)$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{proposition}

Items (iii) and (v) are the precise sense in which library-level constant factors
\emph{relocate but never remove} the optimum.

\begin{theorem}[Geometric convexity]\label{thm:geomconv}
Let $A,q\ge0$, $k_1,k_2>0$ and $w \in [0,1]$. Then for every $\mu$,
\[
C_{A,c,q,\mu}\bigl(k_1^{w}k_2^{1-w}\bigr)
\;\le\; w\,C_{A,c,q,\mu}(k_1) + (1-w)\,C_{A,c,q,\mu}(k_2).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Apply the two-point weighted AM--GM inequality $x^{w}y^{1-w}\le wx+(1-w)y$ to each
nonconstant term, using
$A/(k_1^{w}k_2^{1-w})=(A/k_1)^{w}(A/k_2)^{1-w}$ and
$(k_1^{w}k_2^{1-w})^{\mu-1}=(k_1^{\mu-1})^{w}(k_2^{\mu-1})^{1-w}$.
\end{proof}

Equivalently, the cost is convex in $\log k$. This is the structural reason the
optimum is a \emph{root} of $A/q$: in logarithmic coordinates the cost is a sum of two
exponentials with slopes $-1$ and $\mu-1$, whose minimiser is a weighted geometric
mean of the parameters with weights $(1/\mu,(\mu-1)/\mu)$.

\begin{theorem}[Ordinary convexity fails below $\mu=2$]\label{thm:notconvex}
The function $k \mapsto C_{1,0,1,3/2}(k)=1/k+\sqrt{k}-1$ is not convex on
$(0,\infty)$: with $k_1=4$, $k_2=100$ and midpoint $52$,
\[
\tfrac12\bigl(C(4)+C(100)\bigr) \;<\; C(52).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$C(4)=1.25$ and $C(100)=9.01$, so the average is $5.13$; whereas $\sqrt{52}>7.2$
gives $C(52)>6.2$.
\end{proof}

For $\mu=2$ the cost is convex, so Theorem~\ref{thm:notconvex} isolates a genuinely
sub-quadratic phenomenon: below the quadratic model, convexity survives only
multiplicatively. Optimisation routines assuming convexity in $k$ lose their
guarantees; the same routines applied to $\log k$ remain valid.

\section{Unimodality and the discrete optimum}\label{sec:discrete}

\begin{lemma}[Auxiliary setups]\label{lem:aux}
Let $A,q>0$, $\mu>1$ and $A'(k):=q(\mu-1)k^{\mu}$, so $k$ is the balanced point of
the cost with setup $A'(k)$. Then $A'(k)\le A$ for $0<k\le\kstar$ and $A'(k)\ge A$
for $k \ge \kstar$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Strict unimodality]\label{thm:unimodal}
Let $A,q>0$ and $\mu>1$. Then $C$ is strictly decreasing on $(0,\kstar]$ and
strictly increasing on $[\kstar,\infty)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch (balance shift)]
For the first claim take $0<k_1<k_2\le\kstar$ and set $A':=q(\mu-1)k_2^{\mu}$, so
that $k_2$ is the balanced point of the auxiliary problem. Theorem~\ref{thm:balance}
gives
\[
\frac{A'}{k_2}+qk_2^{\mu-1} \;=\; q\mu k_2^{\mu-1} \;<\; \frac{A'}{k_1}+qk_1^{\mu-1}.
\]
By Lemma~\ref{lem:aux}, $A'\le A$, and the discrepancy $(A-A')/k$ between the true
and auxiliary costs is nonincreasing in $k$, so $(A-A')/k_2 \le (A-A')/k_1$. Writing
$A/k_i=A'/k_i+(A-A')/k_i$ and adding yields $C(k_2)<C(k_1)$. The second claim is
symmetric, with $A':=q(\mu-1)k_1^{\mu}\ge A$ and discrepancy $-(A'-A)/k$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The best integer batch is a neighbour of $\kstar$]\label{thm:integer}
Let $A,q>0$ and $\mu>1$. For every integer $n \ge 1$,
\[
\min\Bigl\{\,C\bigl(\max(1,\lfloor \kstar\rfloor)\bigr),\;
C\bigl(\lceil \kstar\rceil\bigr)\Bigr\} \;\le\; C(n).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
If $n\le\kstar$ then $n\le\lfloor\kstar\rfloor\le\kstar$ and
Theorem~\ref{thm:unimodal} gives $C(\lfloor\kstar\rfloor)\le C(n)$; if $n>\kstar$
then $\lceil\kstar\rceil\le n$ and Theorem~\ref{thm:unimodal} gives
$C(\lceil\kstar\rceil)\le C(n)$. The clamp $\max(1,\cdot)$ matters only when
$\kstar<1$, where the smallest admissible batch is optimal.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Search correctness]\label{cor:search}
Integer ternary search on a bracket $[1,N]$ containing $\lceil\kstar\rceil$ returns
the exact discrete optimum in $O(\log N)$ cost evaluations; strict unimodality
excludes trapping local minima.
\end{corollary}

\section{The batch-versus-solo crossover}\label{sec:crossover}

\begin{definition}\label{def:crossover}
Let $s_1$ be the cost of testing one candidate alone, $c_1$ the flat per-candidate
cost inside a batch and $q>0$ the multiplication-penalty scale. The per-candidate
batch cost for a pool of size $k$ is $B_{\mu}(k) := q(k^{\mu-1}-1)+c_1$, and the
crossover pool size is
\[
M^{*}(\mu) := \left(1+\frac{s_1-c_1}{q}\right)^{\frac{1}{\mu-1}} .
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Root form of the crossover]\label{thm:crossover}
Let $q>0$, $\mu>1$, $k>0$ and suppose $1+(s_1-c_1)/q \ge 0$. Then
\[
B_{\mu}(k) \le s_1 \iff k \le M^{*}(\mu).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$B_{\mu}(k)\le s_1$ is equivalent to $k^{\mu-1}\le (q+s_1-c_1)/q = 1+(s_1-c_1)/q$.
Since $\mu-1>0$, the map $x\mapsto x^{\mu-1}$ is a strictly increasing bijection of
$[0,\infty)$, and $\bigl(M^{*}(\mu)\bigr)^{\mu-1}=1+(s_1-c_1)/q$ because
$\frac{1}{\mu-1}(\mu-1)=1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Schoolbook specialisation]\label{cor:cross2}
$M^{*}(2)=1+(s_1-c_1)/q$: the crossover in the schoolbook word model is linear in
the setup/penalty ratio, and the linearity is an artefact of $\mu=2$. Calibrated to a
measured crossover of $1715$ candidates, the calibration reads $s_1-c_1=1714\,q$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Sub-quadratic arithmetic only delays the reversal]\label{thm:delay}
If $q>0$, $1<\mu\le2$ and $c_1 \le s_1$, then $M^{*}(2) \le M^{*}(\mu)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
With $R:=1+(s_1-c_1)/q \ge 1$ and $1/(\mu-1)\ge1$, monotonicity of $x\mapsto R^{x}$
for $R\ge1$ gives $R^{1}\le R^{1/(\mu-1)}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Divergence of the crossover as $\mu \downarrow 1$]\label{thm:crossdiv}
Let $q>0$, $\mu>1$, $c_1\le s_1$, $M\ge1$ and $R:=1+(s_1-c_1)/q$. If
$(\mu-1)\log M \le \log R$, then $M \le M^{*}(\mu)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The hypothesis is $\log M \le \frac{1}{\mu-1}\log R$, the logarithmic form of
$M \le R^{1/(\mu-1)}$.
\end{proof}

With $R=1715$ and $M=512$ this covers every $\mu \le 1+\log 1715/\log 512
\approx 2.19$, which includes the whole sub-quadratic range and explains why
operation-count measurements up to $k=512$ observed no reversal.

\begin{proposition}[Calibrated instance in the Karatsuba range]\label{prop:calib}
If $s_1-c_1 = 1714\,q$ with $q>0$, then $M^{*}(3/2)=1715^{2}=2\,941\,225$.
\end{proposition}

The reversal measured at $1715$ candidates in the schoolbook model would, with
$\mu=3/2$ arithmetic, occur only beyond $2.9\times10^{6}$ candidates.

\section{Algorithms}

\paragraph{Closed-form batch sizing.} Given $(A,c,q,\mu)$ with $\mu>1$, compute
$\kstar=(A/((\mu-1)q))^{1/\mu}$ and return the better of
$\max(1,\lfloor\kstar\rfloor)$ and $\lceil\kstar\rceil$, which by
Theorem~\ref{thm:integer} is the exact integer optimum. For $\mu \le 1$ report ``no
finite optimum''. Cost: $O(1)$.

\paragraph{Calibration by measurement.} At fixed $\mu$ the model is linear in
$(A,c,q)$ with design rows $(1/k_i,\,1,\,k_i^{\mu-1}-1)$; three or more measurements
determine the parameters by (least-squares) linear solve, and $\mu$ itself may be
fitted by a scalar outer search. Theorem~\ref{thm:collapse} supplies the diagnostic:
rescaled to shape coordinates, all measurements must fall on the single curve
$S_{\mu}$.

\paragraph{Model-free ternary search.} If only black-box measurements are available,
strict unimodality (Theorem~\ref{thm:unimodal}) makes integer ternary search on
$[1,N]$ correct, with $O(\log N)$ evaluations and no parameter knowledge required.

\section{Applications and discussion}

At $\mu=2$ the root law is the economic order quantity formula of inventory theory,
with setup corresponding to ordering cost and penalty to holding cost; the general
law extends it to holding costs growing like a power of the order size, with the
same balance interpretation. More broadly, any amortisation problem of the form
``fixed cost per group, super-linear cost within a group'' is an instance: I/O
blocking, GPU kernel batching, cryptographic batch verification, bulk database
inserts, gradient accumulation. The practically decisive statement is
Corollary~\ref{cor:missize}: within a factor of two of $\kstar$ the overhead is at
most $(\mu-1)/2$ in shape units, so precise tuning is rarely worth the engineering.

Theorem~\ref{thm:collapse} is a sharp falsifiability statement: a single-tier model
has exactly one optimum, so a measured cost curve with two local minima lies outside
the model. The most likely cause is a memory hierarchy, in which the effective
exponent jumps when the working set spills a cache level; real multiplication
libraries also switch algorithms at thresholds, making $\mu$ piecewise constant in
the operand size and hence in $k$.

\section{Future work}

\begin{enumerate}[label=(\arabic*)]
\item \textbf{Cache-tier cost laws and piecewise root optima.} A memory hierarchy
makes the multiplication exponent a step function of the batch size, so the global
cost is a finite gluing of root laws, each valid on its own tier, and the true
optimum is the best of finitely many tier-local optima. The universal-collapse
theorem makes this testable: any measured second local minimum is direct evidence of
a tier boundary.
\item \textbf{Root laws for arbitrary superadditive penalty profiles.} The balance
principle never used the specific form $k^{\mu-1}$, only that the penalty is
multiplicatively convex and the amortised setup is multiplicatively convex with the
opposite slope. For a regularly varying penalty $P(k)$ one expects a unique optimum
wherever the elasticity $kP'(k)/P(k)$ crosses $1$, with the root law as the
constant-index case; FFT multiplication, at $k\log k\log\log k$, lives exactly in
this regime.
\item \textbf{Sharp constant in the flatness bound.} The bound
$(\mu-1)(\theta-1)^{2}/\theta$ is Bernoulli-derived, whereas the true excess
$S_{\mu}(\theta)-\mu$ has exact Taylor coefficient $\mu(\mu-1)/2$ at $\theta=1$;
identifying the largest interval on which the crude bound stays within a fixed factor
of the truth converts the qualitative plateau statement into a quantitative tuning
tolerance.
\item \textbf{Stochastic and heterogeneous streams.} With variable candidate sizes
the effective batch length is random and the object of interest is $\mathbb{E}[C]$;
multiplicative convexity suggests working in logarithmic coordinates.
\item \textbf{Multi-level batching.} Product trees are recursive batchings;
optimising the branching factor at each level is a nested version of the present
problem.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Conclusion}

The per-candidate cost of batching, $A/k+c+q(k^{\mu-1}-1)$, has for every
multiplication exponent $\mu>1$ a unique optimal batch size
$\kstar=(A/((\mu-1)q))^{1/\mu}$ with optimal cost
$c-q+\mu(\mu-1)^{(1-\mu)/\mu}A^{(\mu-1)/\mu}q^{1/\mu}$, degenerating to ``bigger is
always better'' exactly at $\mu=1$. The classical square-root rule is the $\mu=2$
case. The curve collapses to a universal two-parameter shape, the optimum is a
quadratically flat plateau, the cost is multiplicatively but (for $\mu<2$) not
additively convex, the discrete optimum is a neighbour of $\kstar$, and the
batch-versus-solo crossover is a $1/(\mu-1)$-th root of the setup/penalty ratio. The
measured reversal in batch smoothness testing is thereby explained as a property of
schoolbook arithmetic rather than of batching.

